## Emulator args for the local ARM-Mac dev node.
##
## Selected with VMARGS_PATH at launch -- the extended start script reads that
## override before falling back to releases/<vsn>/vm.args, which the dev
## release does not ship ({vm_args, false} in rebar.config's base relx block):
##
##   VMARGS_PATH=$PWD/config/vm.args.dev \
##   RELX_CONFIG_PATH=$PWD/config/sys-dev.config \
##   KAZOO_CONFIG=$PWD/config/config-dev.ini \
##     _build/dev/rel/kazoo/bin/kazoo foreground
##
## Without it EVERY bin/kazoo subcommand dies in awk, not just boot -- the
## script greps this file for the node name and cookie before doing anything.
## Commands that only need identity (remote_console, ping, attach) need
## VMARGS_PATH alone.
##
## Use an ABSOLUTE path to boot. The script's own awk pass reads VMARGS_PATH
## relative to your shell, so `bin/kazoo ping' appears to work with a relative
## one -- but the script then cd's to the release root before exec'ing erlexec,
## and the -args_file it hands the emulator resolves from there instead.

## A longname on a literal IP: deterministic, and no dependency on whatever
## .local hostname the Mac happens to advertise. The debugger attach in
## els_dap's launch.json must name this node exactly.
-name kazoo_apps@127.0.0.1

## MUST match [kazoo_apps] cookie in config/config-dev.ini.
## This value is only the cookie the node *starts* with: once kazoo_apps boots,
## kazoo_apps_init:set_cookie/0 resets the live cookie to the INI's value. If
## the two disagree, remote_console works until kazoo_apps starts and then
## fails authentication for the rest of the node's life.
-setcookie kazoo_dev_cookie

## Kazoo is heavy on both -- the defaults (262144 / 1024) are reached by a
## single node running all whapps plus ecallmgr.
+P 1048576
+Q 65536

## Keep crash reports readable rather than truncated at 1024 chars.
+pc unicode
-env ERL_CRASH_DUMP_SECONDS 0

## Dev only: leave SMP heuristics alone but don't let a busy dispatcher stall
## the shell while stepping through a breakpoint.
+sbwt none
+swt low
